    "result": "Perfect! The bug is fixed. Here's what was happening and how I fixed it:\n\n## Root Cause\nWhen you passed `39.95`, JavaScript's floating-point arithmetic produced `3995.0000000000005` instead of exactly `3995` when converting to cents. The validation was checking `Number.isInteger(sub.price)`, which failed because of this tiny precision error (0.0000000000005).\n\nNetflix (12.99) and Spotify (10.99) worked by luck\u2014they have more favorable floating-point representations in binary.\n\n## Solution\nI added `Math.round()` to both price parsing functions in `src/money.js`:\n\n- **`parseEuros()`** (line 13): Now rounds the result to the nearest integer\n- **`cellCents()`** (line 25): Now rounds the result to the nearest integer\n\nThis handles floating-point precision loss consistently for any price that might encounter it.\n\n\u2705 All 50 tests pass  \n\u2705 Gym ($39.95), Netflix ($12.99), and Spotify ($10.99) all add successfully  \n\u2705 Subscriptions persist correctly",
